q('ILD occurs with Raynaud phenomenon, inflammatory joint symptoms and abnormal nailfold findings. What is the best diagnostic principle?', 'Evaluate a connective tissue disease-associated process rather than assuming idiopathic fibrosis',[
 ('A negative screening ANA excludes every autoimmune cause','Some syndromes need different antibodies and clinical assessment.'),('All extrapulmonary findings are incidental by definition','They can identify the disease mechanism.'),('Every CTD-ILD must have an identical NSIP pattern','Patterns vary, including UIP.')],
 'A lung presentation can precede a systemic diagnosis', '**History and examination guide targeted serology and rheumatology review.**',['Muscle, skin, joint and vascular features can be subtle.','Antibody results need clinical interpretation.'], '**Use joint respiratory-rheumatology assessment.**',['The diagnosis changes immunomodulatory and antifibrotic decisions.','Do not diagnose a full syndrome from one weak nonspecific antibody alone.'],[['Clues','Raynaud/joint/skin findings'],['Testing','Targeted autoimmune evaluation'],['Care','Multidisciplinary']],[CT],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which therapy has a strong ATS recommendation for systemic sclerosis-associated ILD?', 'Mycophenolate',[
 ('Routine high-dose prednisone monotherapy for every patient','This is not the preferred universal strategy and raises renal-crisis concern.'),('No treatment can be considered until end-stage respiratory failure','Risk and progression can justify earlier treatment.'),('An asthma anti-IgE drug as the established first choice','It is not the guideline’s standard SSc-ILD therapy.')],
 'SSc-ILD has disease-specific treatment evidence', '**ATS recommends mycophenolate and conditionally supports several other options.**',['Cyclophosphamide, rituximab, tocilizumab and nintedanib can be relevant in selected settings.','Inflammatory phenotype, extent and progression affect selection.'], '**Monitor infection, counts and organ safety.**',['Use contraception and pregnancy planning for teratogenic drugs.','Combine therapy with longitudinal function and imaging assessment.'],[['Core option','Mycophenolate'],['Other options','Selected immunomodulators/antifibrotic'],['Decision','Risk and progression phenotype']],[SS,CT])
q('Why are high-dose glucocorticoids used cautiously in systemic sclerosis with lung disease?', 'They can increase concern for scleroderma renal crisis',[
 ('They are proven harmless for kidneys in all SSc phenotypes','Renal-crisis risk is important.'),('All CTD-ILDs have exactly the same steroid strategy','Treatment differs by systemic disease.'),('A normal creatinine today removes every future risk','The risk is dynamic.')],
 'An inflammatory-lung strategy can harm another organ', '**Systemic sclerosis requires particular caution with glucocorticoid exposure.**',['Blood pressure and kidney monitoring are important.','Other diseases such as inflammatory myositis can require a different approach.'], '**Choose disease-specific therapy.**',['Do not transfer a generic ILD steroid plan to every CTD.','Assess new hypertension or renal deterioration urgently.'],[['Disease','Systemic sclerosis'],['Concern','Renal crisis'],['Strategy','Minimize unnecessary steroid exposure']],[BS,SS])
q('A rheumatoid arthritis patient has a UIP-pattern ILD. Which statement is correct?', 'The UIP pattern does not automatically make the disease idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis',[
 ('RA cannot produce UIP','It is an important RA-ILD pattern.'),('Every RA patient with dyspnea has only drug toxicity','Infection, cardiac disease and intrinsic ILD are alternatives.'),('All RA-UIP needs the exact PANTHER regimen','That IPF combination is not a generic ILD treatment.')],
 'Pattern and cause must remain separate', '**RA-associated disease can have fibrotic and inflammatory components.**',['Treatment considers systemic activity and pulmonary progression.','Antifibrotic therapy can be relevant in a progressive phenotype.'], '**Use multidisciplinary diagnosis and treatment.**',['Review medicines without assuming every lung lesion is medication induced.','Assess infection before escalating immunosuppression.'],[['Morphology','UIP possible'],['Etiology','Can be RA associated'],['Treatment','Phenotype and progression based']],[CT,PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('ILD is accompanied by mechanic’s hands, inflammatory arthritis and an antisynthetase antibody. What syndrome is suggestive?', 'Antisynthetase syndrome',[
 ('Myositis is impossible unless weakness is already severe','Lung disease can precede overt muscle involvement.'),('A single normal CK excludes every inflammatory-myopathy syndrome','Amyopathic or lung-predominant disease can occur.'),('The antibody means all disease is confined to the joints','The syndrome can be multisystem.')],
 'Look beyond the degree of muscle weakness', '**Anti-Jo-1 and other aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase antibodies define related phenotypes.**',['ILD, arthritis, Raynaud and mechanic’s hands can vary in prominence.','The antibody assay and clinical context matter.'], '**Assess pulmonary severity promptly.**',['Use a respiratory-rheumatology treatment plan.','Monitor both lung and extrapulmonary activity over time.'],[['Clues','Mechanic’s hands and arthritis'],['Antibody','Antisynthetase'],['Lung','Can be dominant or early']],[CT],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has characteristic dermatomyositis skin disease, little weakness, anti-MDA5 antibodies and rapidly worsening gas exchange. What is the key implication?', 'Urgent assessment for rapidly progressive myositis-associated ILD',[
 ('Minimal muscle weakness guarantees mild lung disease','Amyopathic phenotypes can have severe pulmonary disease.'),('Wait months for a routine repeat CK before lung treatment','The respiratory trajectory is urgent.'),('The antibody proves infection cannot coexist','Infection remains an important competing or concurrent cause.')],
 'Muscle and lung severity can be dissociated', '**Anti-MDA5 disease is associated with a high-risk rapidly progressive ILD phenotype.**',['Other organ and inflammatory findings refine risk.','The evidence and intensity of combination treatment require specialist judgment.'], '**Use urgent multidisciplinary care.**',['Assess infection and respiratory support needs.','Do not let a normal CK create false reassurance.'],[['Phenotype','Often little clinical myositis'],['Risk','Rapid pulmonary progression'],['Action','Urgent specialist assessment']],[CT],'clinical_scenario')
q('Persistent migratory peripheral consolidations follow a respiratory illness. Infection and other causes are being assessed. What does an organizing-pneumonia pattern represent?', 'A lung injury-repair pattern that can be cryptogenic or secondary',[
 ('It always proves an ongoing bacterial pneumonia','The pattern is not synonymous with infection.'),('It is always idiopathic without reviewing drugs or autoimmune disease','Secondary causes are important.'),('Steroid response alone permanently establishes the cause','Several processes can transiently respond.')],
 'A histological or radiological pattern is not the full etiological diagnosis', '**Organizing pneumonia can follow infection, drugs, radiation or systemic disease.**',['Malignancy and other mimics require consideration.','The need for tissue depends on confidence and consequences.'], '**Exclude important infection before immunosuppression.**',['Treat the driver when identifiable.','Follow for relapse and reconsider atypical nonresponse.'],[['Pattern','Organization after lung injury'],['Cause','Cryptogenic or secondary'],['Care','Cause evaluation and monitored treatment']],[FL,CT],'clinical_scenario')
q('An acute febrile hypoxemic illness has diffuse opacities and BAL eosinophils 35%, while initial blood eosinophils were normal. Which diagnosis is supported after exclusions?', 'Acute eosinophilic pneumonia',[
 ('Normal initial blood eosinophils exclude eosinophilic lung disease','BAL eosinophilia can precede a blood rise.'),('Pure cardiogenic edema is established by diffuse opacities alone','The marked alveolar eosinophilia suggests another mechanism.'),('Idiopathic disease is proven without medication or infection review','Secondary eosinophilic causes must be excluded.')],
 'The relevant compartment may reveal disease before the blood', '**BAL eosinophilia above about 25% supports an acute eosinophilic pneumonia pattern.**',['New inhaled exposures or smoking changes can be clues.','Drugs, parasites and other infections require context-directed evaluation.'], '**Stabilize and use appropriate specialist treatment.**',['Corticosteroid response can be rapid in suitable cases.','Do not delay infection assessment or respiratory support.'],[['Onset','Acute'],['BAL','Marked eosinophilia'],['Blood','Can initially be normal']],[FL],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with asthma has weeks of symptoms, peripheral pulmonary consolidations and substantial blood eosinophilia. Which pattern is suggestive?', 'Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia',[
 ('All eosinophilia in asthma is uncomplicated airway disease','Parenchymal opacities and systemic symptoms broaden the differential.'),('A positive ANA alone proves IPF','This does not fit the eosinophilic pattern.'),('Steroids can be prescribed without excluding parasites or drugs','Important secondary causes require review.')],
 'A subacute eosinophilic parenchymal process differs from an ordinary asthma flare', '**The course is usually more prolonged than acute eosinophilic pneumonia.**',['Relapses after steroid reduction are common.','EGPA and other systemic disorders must be considered when extrapulmonary signs occur.'], '**Establish the cause and a follow-up plan.**',['Use blood, BAL and imaging together when indicated.','Minimize long-term steroid harm and reassess recurrent disease.'],[['Course','Weeks rather than abrupt hours'],['Pattern','Peripheral consolidation/eosinophilia'],['Follow-up','Relapse and alternative-cause review']],[FL],'clinical_scenario')
q('A young smoker has upper-zone nodules and irregular cysts with recurrent pneumothorax. Which diagnosis is suggestive?', 'Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis',[
 ('Typical LAM as the best upper-nodular smoking pattern','LAM more often produces diffuse thin-walled cysts in the appropriate phenotype.'),('Simple isolated asthma','It does not explain nodulocystic destruction.'),('A purely pleural effusion disorder','The primary abnormality is parenchymal.')],
 'Smoking-related nodulocystic disease has a recognizable distribution', '**PLCH can evolve from nodules to irregular cysts.**',['Single-system pulmonary disease differs from multisystem LCH.','Diagnosis can require tissue and molecular assessment in selected cases.'], '**Stop tobacco and other inhaled exposures.**',['Cessation is first-line care in isolated pulmonary LCH.','Assess pneumothorax, pulmonary hypertension and progression.'],[['Exposure','Strong smoking association'],['CT','Nodules plus irregular cysts'],['First intervention','Exposure cessation']],[LC],'clinical_scenario')
q('A woman has diffuse thin-walled lung cysts, recurrent pneumothorax and a chylous effusion. Which disease should be evaluated?', 'Lymphangioleiomyomatosis',[
 ('UIP-pattern IPF as the typical CT finding','UIP is a reticular/honeycomb fibrotic pattern rather than this diffuse cystic syndrome.'),('Ordinary bacterial bronchitis','It does not account for diffuse cysts and chyle.'),('A normal VEGF-D would exclude every LAM case','A low result is not fully sensitive.')],
 'Diffuse cystic disease needs a phenotype-specific assessment', '**LAM can be sporadic or associated with tuberous sclerosis.**',['VEGF-D can support diagnosis and avoid biopsy in an appropriate setting.','Renal angiomyolipoma is another helpful clue.'], '**Use expert diagnostic and treatment pathways.**',['Sirolimus is indicated for selected abnormal or declining lung function and other manifestations.','Plan pneumothorax prevention and avoid unproven hormonal manipulation.'],[['Pattern','Diffuse thin-walled cysts'],['Clues','Chyle/angiomyolipoma'],['Targeted therapy','Selected mTOR inhibition']],[LA],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient on long-term nitrofurantoin develops progressive cough and diffuse interstitial abnormalities. What is the appropriate principle?', 'Consider drug-induced lung injury and review withdrawal while excluding other causes',[
 ('A prescribed drug cannot cause chronic ILD','Several medicines can.'),('Every new infiltrate is definitely toxicity without testing','Infection, autoimmune disease and other causes remain possible.'),('Continue the suspected drug indefinitely because fibrosis is already present','Ongoing exposure can worsen injury.')],
 'Medication history is part of every ILD assessment', '**Timing, exposure duration and alternative explanations inform causality.**',['Amiodarone and other agents are additional important examples.','A specific imaging pattern is rarely fully diagnostic of drug cause.'], '**Coordinate a safe replacement for the original indication.**',['Assess severity and the need for supportive or anti-inflammatory treatment.','Follow recovery because structural injury may persist.'],[['Possible driver','Long-term medicine'],['Action','Causality assessment and exposure review'],['Follow-up','Clinical, physiological and imaging response']],[FL,PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('Basal fibrosis occurs with pleural plaques and a substantial occupational asbestos history. What is the best interpretation?', 'Evaluate asbestos-related disease, recognizing that plaques mark exposure rather than proving malignancy',[
 ('Pleural plaques alone prove mesothelioma','They are not equivalent to cancer.'),('Every basal UIP-like pattern is idiopathic regardless of exposure','The occupational history can identify a cause.'),('A normal symptom history excludes all exposure effects','Disease can emerge after a long latency.')],
 'Exposure markers and parenchymal disease require integrated assessment', '**Asbestosis can resemble other fibrotic patterns.**',['Smoking and asbestos jointly affect lung-cancer risk.','A new effusion or suspicious pleural mass needs separate evaluation.'], '**Document the exposure and evaluate complications.**',['Use occupational and ILD expertise.','Do not infer a malignant diagnosis from benign plaques alone.'],[['Plaques','Exposure marker'],['Fibrosis','Possible asbestosis in context'],['Malignancy','Separate risk and diagnostic assessment']],[PF],'clinical_scenario')
q('Nonnecrotizing lung granulomas occur in a worker with aerospace-metal exposure. Which test can help distinguish chronic beryllium disease from sarcoidosis?', 'Beryllium lymphocyte proliferation testing',[
 ('Serum ACE alone as an exclusive discriminator','ACE is nonspecific.'),('A negative routine bacterial culture alone','It does not assess beryllium sensitization.'),('Thyroid antibody testing','It does not resolve this occupational granulomatous differential.')],
 'A sarcoid-like biopsy can have an occupational immune cause', '**BeLPT identifies beryllium-specific sensitization under validated criteria.**',['Sensitization alone is not identical to established chronic lung disease.','Exposure history and lung findings remain necessary.'], '**Use occupational-lung specialist assessment.**',['Avoid continued hazardous exposure.','Do not label all nonnecrotizing granulomas idiopathic sarcoidosis.'],[['Exposure','Beryllium-containing work'],['Test','BeLPT'],['Integration','Sensitization plus clinical lung evidence']],[BE],'clinical_scenario')
